Bob is right in the ordinary sense: at -10 °C water is frozen (solid ice). Alice is also right about the standard freezing point: pure water at normal pressure freezes at 0 °C. The apparent contradiction comes from treating “freezes at 0 °C” as if it meant water can only be solid at exactly 0 °C. Freezing is a phase transition that begins at 0 °C; below that, the water is already ice. So both statements are true, just about different things. [end of text]


